  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nCephalosporins / Ceclor\n\nChief Complaint:\naortic valvular mass\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nMs. ___ is a ___ year old female with no significant past \nmedical history who presented to ___ in \n___ for elective pelvic organ prolapse surgery w/ \nbladder sling on ___.  The surgery was uneventful, but \npost-operatively, she complained of chest tightness and received \na TTE in work-up, which showed an aortic valvular mass measuring \n1.3 cm, which was later confirmed by ___.  The patient also \nreceived a stress MIBI, which was negative for ischemia.  \nPatient was worked up for endocarditis with serial blood \ncultures, all of which are no growth to date.  Patient, however, \ndid receive pre-operative cipro prior to admission.  She \nremained afebrile throughout admission with no fever, fatigue, \nmurmur, chest pain, headache, dizziness, visual changes, SOB, \nabdominal pain, or distal hand/feet pain.  No exam findings \nsuggestive of distal emboli.  Nonetheless, out of concern for \npossible endocarditis, she was given prophylactic vanc, cipro \nand gent.  She was transferred to ___ for further work-up and \nevaluation of aortic valve mass.\n.  \nOn arrival to the floor, VS: 98.5, BP 160/94, HR 71, RR 18, O2 \nsat 100% (RA).  patient did not complain of any discomforts.  \nShe has been experiencing some sensations of irregular heart \nbeats and on telemetry is noted to have several episodes of \nsustained trigemony.  No chest pain, dizziness, even with \nsquating.\n.  \nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS  \nOn review of systems, she denies any prior history of stroke, \nTIA, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, bleeding at the \ntime of surgery, myalgias, joint pains, cough, hemoptysis, black \nstools or red stools. She denies recent fevers, chills or \nrigors. She denies exertional buttock or calf pain. All of the \nother review of systems were negative.  \n.  \nCardiac review of systems is notable for absence of dyspnea on \nexertion, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, orthopnea, ankle edema, \nsyncope or presyncope.  Has been experiencing some palpitations \nlately.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nPrimary Reason for Hospitalization:  Ms. ___ is a ___ with \nno significant past medical history who presents from OSH with \nan echo finding of a discrete 1.3cm mass on here aortic valve, \nhere for further evaluation.\n\nActive Diagnoses:\n# Aortic Mass.  A 1.3cm round mass is seen on an aortic valve \nleaflet on TTE from OSH on ___.  Per patient, she had an \necho in ___ that did not show evidence of this mass, so it is \nlikely new since then as this mass would be difficult to miss.  \nPatient has been asymptomatic up until this hospitalization with \nno chest pain, SOB, dizziness, lightheadedness.  She has been \nafebrile, no leukocytosis, no physical exam findings of \nendocarditis ___, Oslers, splinter hemorrhages).  Since \nfinding out about this mass, she feels more symptoms of \npalpitations, which has been confirmed by EKG and telemetry.  \nDdx based on appearance on echo includes fibroelastoma, \nendocarditis, myxoma or lipoma.  Fibroelastoma seems most likely \ngiven appearance and location of tumor and lack of clinical \nsymptoms of endocarditis.  She has no predisposing factors for \nendocarditis (although does endorse getting a crown on a tooth 3 \nmonths ago), no distal emboli or finding suggestive of \nendocarditis.   Troponins were negative, so patient is not \nhaving cardiac event.  CRP 8.5, ESR 37, which are slightly \nelevated, but in the setting of being post-op.  Patient was \noriginally placed on vancomycin and getamicin at OSH for \nsuspicion of endocarditis.  These antibiotics were not continued \nhere because patient has none of the Duke criteria and very low \nlikelihood of having endocarditis.  She was started on ASA325 to \nminimize risk of thromboemboli.  It was felt that the risk of \nthromboemboli was not enough to warrant anticoagulation with \nwarfarin.\n\n#s/p bladder sling.  Patient underwent surgery on ___ at \n___ Urology and will follow-up with urologist \nthere.  She was prescribed 7 days of ciprofloxacin \npost-operative, which was continued here on admission.  She was \ndischarged with a prescription for one remaining day of \nciprofloxacin. \n \n# Ventricular trigeminy.  Patient was monitored on telemetry and \nfound to frequently have ventricular trigeminy.  She is \nsymptomatic from this arrhythmia.  Per her own report, perhaps \nshe feels it more now because she constant thinks about it, but \ncould have been feeling mild symptoms for a while.  Palpitations \nwere especially severe after electrolyte repletion overnight.  \nHer electrolytes were repleted, and she was counseled to \nfollow-up on this issue as an outpatient.  If palpitations \ncontinue and bother her symptomatically, she can ask outpatient \ncardiologist or PCP for beta blockade.\n\nChronic Diagnoses:\n#  GERD.  Patient did not experiencing reflux symptoms this \nadmission and was continued on protonix 40mg PO daily per home \nregimen.\n\nTransitional Issues:\nPatient's OSH CD of echo and stress MIBI images were uploaded \nonto PACS.\nShe will follow-up with Dr. ___ as an outpatient to \nmonitor size of mass and decide future management.\nEMERGENCY CONTACT:  ___ (husband) ___\n\nADDENDUM - cardiac catheterization was performed on ___ - \nthere was no significant coronary artery disease seen. The \npatient will have surgery under the care of Dr. ___.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPrimary Diagnosis: Aortic Valve Fibroelastoma",
